--TEST--
Core\Jwt::signObject and Core\Jwt::sign write the same header for the same key and the same bag, and refuse the same X25519 pair — `rule:security/algorithm-comes-from-the-key`, `rule:security/protocol-roster`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement between the two members that sign. The question is asked of the
// half that is not the payload — the header and the key — because that half is
// the same rule for both, and a second member that grew its own header or its
// own answer about which kinds sign would still look right on its own line.
Core\Crypto\KeyPair $pair = Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair(Core\Crypto\KeyKind::Ed25519);

string $table = Core\Jwt::sign(
    ["sub" => "ada"],
    15m,
    $pair,
    {kid: "k1", typ: "at+jwt", embedKey: true}
);
string $object = Core\Jwt::signObject(
    Core\Arr::shapeAs<{sub: string}>(["sub" => "ada"]),
    15m,
    $pair,
    {kid: "k1", typ: "at+jwt", embedKey: true}
);

string $written = Core\Encoding::decodeText(
    Core\Encoding::fromBase64Url(Core\Str::split($table, ".")[0]),
    Core\Charset::Utf8
);
if (Core\Str::split($table, ".")[0] == Core\Str::split($object, ".")[0]) {
    echo "one header, byte for byte", "\n";
} else {
    echo "two headers: ", $written, " and ", Core\Str::split($object, ".")[0], "\n";
}

// The header itself, around the one part of it that is drawn fresh every run:
// the embedded public half sits between `alg` and `kid`, because the members
// are written sorted and nothing about a random key can move them.
if (
    Core\Str::startsWith($written, "{\"alg\":\"EdDSA\",\"jwk\":{\"crv\":\"Ed25519\",\"kty\":\"OKP\",\"x\":\"")
    && Core\Str::endsWith($written, "\"},\"kid\":\"k1\",\"typ\":\"at+jwt\"}")
) {
    echo "sorted, with the pair's public half between `alg` and `kid`", "\n";
} else {
    echo "the header is ", $written, "\n";
}

// `embedKey` needs no refusal here, which is the one place the two members
// differ: `sign` takes a shared secret that has no public half, and this one
// takes a pair or nothing. So the option that is a bug there is always a
// header member here, and the `jwk` above is it.

// The kind that signs nothing is a bug under either member, and the sentence
// names the member that was called rather than the one that owns the table.
try {
    Core\Jwt::signObject(
        Core\Arr::shapeAs<{sub: string}>(["sub" => "ada"]),
        15m,
        Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair(Core\Crypto\KeyKind::X25519)
    );
    echo "an X25519 pair signed a structured token", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $signsNothing) {
    echo $signsNothing->message, "\n";
}

// And the algorithm still comes from the key alone: a P256 pair writes ES256
// through this member exactly as it does through `sign`.
echo Core\Encoding::decodeText(
    Core\Encoding::fromBase64Url(
        Core\Str::split(
            Core\Jwt::signObject(
                Core\Arr::shapeAs<{sub: string}>(["sub" => "ada"]),
                15m,
                Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair(Core\Crypto\KeyKind::P256)
            ),
            "."
        )[0]
    ),
    Core\Charset::Utf8
), "\n";
--EXPECT--
one header, byte for byte
sorted, with the pair's public half between `alg` and `kid`
Core\Jwt::signObject(): an `X25519` pair signs nothing — it is a key for Core\Crypto::agree alone. A `P256` pair signs ES256, an `Ed25519` pair EdDSA, and the two RSA kinds RS256 and PS256.
{"alg":"ES256","typ":"JWT"}
